From: primeinc <noreply@primeinc.com>
Subject: [PATCH] Cygwin: make a duplicate identical root mount a no-op, not
 a fatal

user_info::initialize() holds spinlock sversion while it calls
mountinfo.init(false) and then internal_getpwsid().  init(false) adds "/"
via create_root_entry() before the account lookup runs, so the shared
mount table is populated while the lock is still held.

If that lookup blocks longer than SPINLOCK_WAIT (15s) -- an unreachable
domain controller is one way, but any stall does -- a second process
waiting on the spinlock gives up and proceeds as the initializer:

  spinlock.h:  if (val == -1)
                 val = 0;  /* Timed out.  We'll initialize things ourselves. */

It then runs mount_info::init(false) against the already-populated table.
init() appends and never resets nmounts, so create_root_entry() calls
add_item() for a "/" that already exists and is MOUNT_IMMUTABLE.  Without
MOUNT_OVERRIDE that returns EPERM, and create_root_entry() turns it into

  add_item ("\??\C:\...", "/", ...) failed, errno 1

The second add is byte-identical to the first: same native path, same
flags.  Treat it like the MOUNT_OVERRIDE case and break, which falls
through to the existing "write over an existing mount item" path.  That
re-inits the identical entry and, importantly, still sets root_idx --
returning early instead skips it and trades this fatal for the
root_idx < 0 fatal a few lines further down in init().

Reproduced and verified with two DLLs built from this tree differing only
in this hunk, with a 20s Sleep() injected into user_info::initialize()
to make the stall deterministic: stock process B dies with add_item
failed errno 1; patched, both processes start normally.

Source: https://github.com/primeinc/msys2-runtime/commit/fix/add-item-idempotent-root
(2026-09-04)

---
 winsup/cygwin/mount.cc | 11 +++++++++++
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+)

diff --git a/winsup/cygwin/mount.cc b/winsup/cygwin/mount.cc
--- a/winsup/cygwin/mount.cc
+++ b/winsup/cygwin/mount.cc
@@ -1665,6 +1665,17 @@ mount_info::add_item (const char *native, const char *posix,
 	      mountflags |= MOUNT_IMMUTABLE;
 	      break;
 	    }
+	  else if (!strcmp (mount[i].native_path, nativetmp)
+		   && mount[i].flags == mountflags)
+	    /* Re-adding a byte-identical immutable entry is a no-op, not an
+	       error.  A second process can run mount_info::init(false) against
+	       an already-populated shared mount table: the spinlock in
+	       user_info::initialize gives up after SPINLOCK_WAIT and lets a
+	       waiter proceed as the initializer, so create_root_entry runs a
+	       second time and would otherwise api_fatal on its own identical
+	       "/" entry.  Nothing is being overridden, so there is nothing to
+	       refuse.  */
+	    break;
 	  else
 	    {
 	      set_errno (EPERM);
--
2.14.x

